   "Evidence type": "Experience",
   "Bottom line": "Small, quick-to-read signals and one-line copy can move conversion a lot, and saved items plus timely pushes close the habit loop.",
   "What they did": "Etsy added one line to the cart, 'Etsy offsets carbon emissions from every delivery', and saw a big conversion lift they didn't expect. They show 'signals and nudges' (for example 'only one left', fast-reply sellers) and buyer review photos so shoppers can decide quickly among 100 million unique items. For retention they used the habit loop around Favorites: an updates feed plus push notifications when a favorited item goes on sale or is about to sell out. Teams run with five leads: product, engineering, design, insights (research and analytics) and marketing.",
   "Results": "On the cart text line: 'we just saw huge uplift that we really, really didn't expect' (no figure given). Tim agreed with the stat that '80% of experiments fail'. Lenny's intro: Etsy grew from 'around 500 million in GMV to over 13 billion in GMV' during Tim's time there.",
   "Takeaways": "One line about values in the cart lifted conversion; Short, glanceable signals help people choose among millions; Favorites feed a habit loop of trigger and reward; Buyer photos build trust when there's no known brand; Retention tests need 30, 60 or 90 days; Use the buyer experience to steer what sellers do",
   "Ideas for OnCourse": "Paywall: A/B test one plain line of copy that says what OnCourse stands for (for example explanations written by doctors who cleared NEET-PG) next to the buy button. Notifications: turn bookmarked or wrong-answer MCQs into triggers ('5 questions you got wrong last week are due for revision') with a link straight into Practice. Practice: show short signals on each question or set ('asked in NEET-PG 2023', '62% got this wrong') so students can quickly see why it matters.",
   "Status": "Read",
   "Added": "2026-11-11",
   "Image": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/n4hRs2FsRug/hqdefault.jpg",
   "Duration": 4317,
   "Times": "exact",
   "Key points": [
    {
     "title": "One line about values in the cart lifted conversion",
     "detail": "Etsy added a single line to the cart: 'Etsy offsets carbon emissions from every delivery.' Tim says 'we just saw huge uplift that we really, really didn't expect' (no figure given). He thinks about effort against reward, and a one-line copy change that reads 'almost like marketing copy' can have an outsized effect. For OnCourse, test one plain line near the buy button about what the app stands for.",
     "t": 2027
    },
    {
     "title": "Short, glanceable signals help people choose among millions",
     "detail": "With over 100 million unique items, Etsy leans on 'signals and nudges' from behavioural economics. Snippets like 'there is actually only one of these' or a badge for sellers who reply to messages fast put buried facts right into the buyer's decision. Tim calls these 'quick summaries, the easy glanceable information'. For OnCourse, short facts on a question or set (asked in a past exam, how many got it wrong) could do the same job.",
     "t": 1818
    },
    {
     "title": "Favorites feed a habit loop of trigger and reward",
     "detail": "A favorite shows intent, so Etsy uses it as a trigger. When a favorited item goes on sale or is about to sell out, Etsy says so in an 'updates feed' and through push notifications. Tim calls that 'a pretty great notification to get', because it's about something the user already chose. Bookmarked or wrongly answered MCQs could be OnCourse's version of favorites.",
     "t": 2566
    },
    {
     "title": "Buyer photos build trust when there's no known brand",
     "detail": "Etsy buyers often buy from one-person shops they have never heard of. Reviews matter more there, especially 'buyer review photos' that show the item in a real hand or home, so the next buyer can judge size and colour. This has 'long been a track that has been very fruitful'. Proof from real peers matters most when the maker is unknown.",
     "t": 1753
    },
    {
     "title": "Retention tests need 30, 60 or 90 days",
     "detail": "After the 2020 surge of new buyers, Etsy shifted to driving frequency. That pushed an A/B-testing culture 'out of our comfort zone': instead of a purchase in one visit, you ask whether people come back in 30, 60 or 90 days. Tim also admits that testing nearly everything can miss the point for bigger new bets. Judge streak or reminder changes over weeks, not one session.",
     "t": 1060
    },
    {
     "title": "Use the buyer experience to steer what sellers do",
     "detail": "Etsy tells sellers what helps, backed by data (for example, more photos lead to more sales), and changes the advice by season. It also 'uses the buyer experience to signal what matters'. When search highlights photos or fast replies, sellers see what they need to improve. Lenny adds that Airbnb pushed Instant Book by showing only instantly bookable listings by default in search.",
     "t": 1561
    }
   ],
