   "Evidence type": "Experience",
   "Bottom line": "Healthy design teams share rough work early in an open feed, say what they will do each week, and cut features nobody uses.",
   "What they did": "At Campsite, Brian's team 'calls their shot': Monday kickoff, Friday recap and a daily bullet list in Slack, plus 'radiating intent' (I'm doing this, speak up if not). They built Linear and GitHub issue creation because customers asked, moved the button out of an overflow menu when nobody used it, then deleted it, and zero people complained. He shares design updates like tweets in a feed so the team builds 'ambient awareness', which at Facebook (Pixel Cloud) helped him spot related work and duplicates. When asking for feedback he says what he wants feedback on and what he doesn't.",
   "Results": "Issue creation: 'nobody used it… a handful' of issues even after making the button prominent; after deleting it 'zero people complained'.",
   "Takeaways": "What users ask for isn't always what they use; Say what feedback you don't want; Share rough work like tweets to build ambient awareness; Sharing gets scarier as the audience grows; Call your shot, then radiate intent; Pick the format for the audience",
   "Ideas for OnCourse": "Design process: Post every rough OnCourse exploration (including discarded ones) to one shared channel, short and unpolished, so PMs and engineers see the process. Design process: When sharing for feedback, add one line on what you want comments on and one on what you don't. Home: Check PostHog usage for each Home card or entry point and remove ones almost nobody taps.",
   "Status": "Read",
   "Added": "2026-11-11",
   "Tools": [
    "Campsite",
    "GitHub"
   ],
   "Image": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/njO9OJTDSGM/hqdefault.jpg",
   "Duration": 2851,
   "Times": "approximate",
   "Key points": [
    {
     "title": "What users ask for isn't always what they use",
     "detail": "Customers asked Campsite to create Linear and GitHub issues from a post, and the team wanted it too. They built it with full syncing, but 'nobody used it… a handful' of issues. They moved the button out of a three-dot overflow menu onto every post, and still nobody used it. They deleted it and 'zero people complained'. Brian's rule: 'everything added to an interface dilutes everything else', so check usage and cut. For OnCourse, look at PostHog taps on each Home entry point and remove the ones almost nobody uses.",
     "t": 719
    },
    {
     "title": "Say what feedback you don't want",
     "detail": "The usual advice is to say what feedback you want. Brian adds the opposite: say what you don't want. His examples: 'I don't want comments on these icons', 'I know this color is wrong, please don't talk about it.' This means thinking about who you want feedback from and what context they need before you post. It keeps feedback focused and saves the reviewer's time.",
     "t": 2239
    },
    {
     "title": "Share rough work like tweets to build ambient awareness",
     "detail": "You don't read every tweet, but scrolling a feed leaves 'breadcrumbs' about what people are doing. Brian wants design updates shared the same way: short, unpolished, and fine to scroll past. At Facebook he browsed the internal tool Pixel Cloud daily from the payments team. He spotted other designers building Commerce things and offered work they could reuse, '1 plus 1 equals 3'. Teams on Campsite also find duplicate work this way. Ridd adds that discarded explorations are often the most useful thing to see.",
     "t": 2319
    },
    {
     "title": "Sharing gets scarier as the audience grows",
     "detail": "A five-person design team with shared trust will post scribbles and rough screenshots. Once PMs, engineers and the CEO join as viewers, people get anxious about showing unpolished work. Brian says remote work made this worse, so designers retreat into Slack DMs. He finds many design leaders want more work-in-progress shared but won't ask, because it costs 'social and political capital'. His view: if a leader won't put 'skin in the game' and say 'this is a thing we do now', the culture won't change.",
     "t": 1759
    },
    {
     "title": "Call your shot, then radiate intent",
     "detail": "Campsite does a Monday kickoff, a Friday recap and a daily bullet list in a Slack updates channel. Saying what you will do in front of peers forces you to be specific, instead of 'just working on this project'. 'Radiating intent' means saying 'I am going to do this, speak up if you don't want me to', rather than asking permission. Brian says this small shift is what lets great teams move fast. A third habit, 'everybody shovels snow', means doing grunt work like tidying files and bug-hunting preview builds.",
     "t": 1199
    },
    {
     "title": "Pick the format for the audience",
     "detail": "Brian's quick guide: executives probably need a deck, PMs a doc, another designer a screenshot or prototype, and an engineer a prototype. He says you learn this by noticing when a different format got better results with a certain person. Elsewhere he warns that flashy landing pages with Bento grids often leave people not knowing what the product does. Campsite built its landing page in 24 hours, focused on words you read as you scroll.",
     "t": 2679
    }
   ],
   "Chapters": [
    {
     "t": 0,
     "title": "Intro and why Brian started Campsite",
     "summary": "How Facebook's culture of sharing early, and GitHub's siloed remote teams, gave Brian the idea for Campsite."
    },
    {
     "t": 159,
     "title": "De-risking the jump to founder",
     "summary": "Nights and weekends, then a 3-month leave of absence spent talking to customers Monday and Tuesday and building the rest of the week."
    },
    {
     "t": 359,
     "title": "Lessons from Spectrum",
     "summary": "Spectrum grew to hundreds of thousands of users after the team stopped working on it, and how hard it is to know when to quit."
    },
    {
     "t": 639,
     "title": "Cutting features without fear",
     "summary": "Why Spectrum didn't cut enough, and how Campsite built, promoted, then deleted its Linear and GitHub issue integration."
    },
    {
     "t": 919,
     "title": "Advice for designers who want to found",
     "summary": "Start a side project and keep it going for three months; the story of Brian's Staff Design interview series."
    },
    {
     "t": 1159,
     "title": "Campsite's design culture",
     "summary": "Calling your shot, 'everybody shovels snow' and radiating intent."
    },
    {
     "t": 1399,
     "title": "Design founder versus founder",
     "summary": "Red flags before product-market fit (custom icons, rebrands, typefaces) and why Campsite's landing page took 24 hours."
    },
    {
     "t": 1719,
     "title": "The sharing anxiety problem",
     "summary": "Why sharing work in progress gets scarier as more people from other teams can see it."
    },
    {
     "t": 1919,
     "title": "What healthy design cultures do",
     "summary": "Shared trust and context, and why design leaders must spend social capital to change how their team shares."
    },
    {
     "t": 2159,
     "title": "Getting better feedback",
     "summary": "Setting context, saying what feedback you want, and saying what you don't want."
    },
    {
     "t": 2319,
     "title": "Ambient awareness",
     "summary": "Sharing like a feed, Pixel Cloud at Facebook, creative serendipity and spotting duplicate work."
    },
    {
     "t": 2679,
     "title": "Choosing a format, and the vision",
     "summary": "Deck, doc, screenshot or prototype depending on audience, and Campsite's goal of faster design iteration."
    }
   ],
