   "Key points": [
    {
     "title": "Build the playground on the production app and design system",
     "detail": "Harsha took Pocket FM's web app and removed the backend, analytics and ad SDKs. That left a light 'skeleton' with the real design system components wired in. He asked the tech team for the design system repo, and the first version took 'less than a week'. He chose this over building from scratch because 'there's an entire front-end team' who already built the screens. Later they did the same with the native iOS app.",
     "t": 386
    },
    {
     "title": "Prototype with real content, not lorem ipsum",
     "detail": "The playground pulls real show trailers and metadata through APIs documented in skills. Harsha says 'we're a content company and content is our game and we can't mock it every time'. Real data shows true description lengths and edge cases. For OnCourse, prototypes should use real questions, explanations and video titles, because long medical text breaks layouts.",
     "t": 1230
    },
    {
     "title": "Treat designers as users: setup and sharing are skills",
     "detail": "Harsha watched a designer struggle with GitHub, so he wrote an 'onboarding' skill. It checks the Mac, Xcode tools, Homebrew and node, and GitHub access. It creates a per-designer branch saved in a config file and starts the server. A 'push my work' skill commits, opens a PR to the QA branch, waits for the VPN-only deploy, returns a link and posts in Slack. The shared CLAUDE.md tells agents to use Figma words (auto layout, frames, fill, hug) and to 'be calm and learning oriented'.",
     "t": 1335
    },
    {
     "title": "Live knobs and pinned comments replace re-prompting",
     "detail": "Every demo gets DialKit sliders, so a designer can change gap, width or animation delay directly. Harsha says asking the agent each time 'is wasting tokens and wasting time'. Agentation gives a Figma-like cursor to pin comments on elements. A background 'watch mode' agent picks up each pin and makes the change live, with no copy and paste. He uses a faster model for these quick loops.",
     "t": 1652
    },
    {
     "title": "A side agent turns repeated corrections into shared learnings",
     "detail": "On every push, a script reads the chat transcript and pulls out 'unsaid rules'. Examples: the button defaults to medium noise texture and must be passed 'noise none', and 'don't render the iOS status bar from Figma'. These get appended to a shared learnings file in GitHub, so every designer's agent knows them. If a rule keeps coming up, Harsha moves it into a skill.",
     "t": 3550
    },
    {
     "title": "A Mobbin skill grounds agents in real UX patterns",
     "detail": "Harsha built one screen, then asked Codex to 'look at Mobbin for references and make the password screen'. It studied real password screens before building the next steps. The skill knows Pocket FM is an entertainment app, so it filters for apps like Spotify. He can also ask it to 'look for something fresh' from another industry. He says the answers are better than asking a model what a password screen needs.",
     "t": 2038
    }
   ],
