   "Evidence type": "Experience",
   "Bottom line": "Good handoff explains why, every state and edge case, and motion as a storyboard, all in one clearly marked spec; teams designing in code now hand off a branch, PR or preview link instead.",
   "What they did": "Figma-based teams keep a separate 'specs' page with only 'ready for dev' sections, an overview and jobs-to-be-done at the top, states, hover/click interactions, long/short text, localization, empty and error states, odd spacing called out explicitly, accessibility screen-reader order, and dated update pages when specs change; one uses a spacing-measure component mapped to size tokens, another writes a grid of every permutation of a side panel and the functionality on-screen because PRDs drift. Firefox splits 'intended design' from what ships in release one after talking to engineering; one designer drew a motion storyboard with in-between keyframes, exact pixel math (pill expands to 343px) and fade timings. Others share a Loom walkthrough plus a live prototype link, or use Magic Patterns to get to 70% for alignment then go back to Figma for the source-of-truth specs. Shopify and Intercom designers instead save to a branch, share a live link with in-browser comments, and open a draft PR plus an experimental build that engineers fork.",
   "Results": "Intercom designer: engineers often say \"Oh, great. I can use 80% of that\" when handed a coded prototype PR. No measured outcomes.",
   "Takeaways": "Code handoff: branch, preview link and draft PR; Keep one clearly marked 'ready for dev' spec; Put final requirements on the design itself; Storyboard motion with keyframes and exact numbers; Explain the why, every state and odd spacing; Split the intended design from release one",
   "Ideas for OnCourse": "Design process: hand off each OnCourse screen as a draft PR plus a preview build, with a short spec note covering why, every state (empty, error, loading, offline) and long Hindi/English text. Practice: write the answer reveal and next-question transition as a keyframe storyboard with timings before engineers build it. Paywall: list every permutation (trial, expired, renewal, discount) in one grid so nothing is missed.",
   "Status": "Read",
   "Added": "2026-11-11",
   "Tools": [
    "Figma",
    "Jira",
    "Loom",
    "Magic Patterns",
    "Principle",
    "GitHub"
   ],
   "Image": "https://i.ytimg.com/vi/CYPeIjgIfOE/hqdefault.jpg",
   "Duration": 1822,
   "Times": "approximate",
   "Key points": [
    {
     "title": "Code handoff: branch, preview link and draft PR",
     "detail": "A Shopify designer saves work to a branch and shares a live link where people leave comments in the browser. Engineers can fork his last commit and 'think with the same materials' instead of translating Figma. An Intercom designer runs a skill that opens a draft PR and an experimental build link. Engineers often say 'Oh, great. I can use 80% of that.' This fits OnCourse, where prototypes are built in the real app.",
     "t": 1614
    },
    {
     "title": "Keep one clearly marked 'ready for dev' spec",
     "detail": "A Slack designer keeps a separate 'specs' page apart from scratch work, with every section marked ready for dev. She adds small notes like 'edit and remove only shows up if you have permissions'. Another designer uses the green ready-for-dev flag as the only signal and greys out older ideas. Both say they never want to assume engineers 'can read my mind'.",
     "t": 517
    },
    {
     "title": "Put final requirements on the design itself",
     "detail": "A Lattice designer writes the functionality on the screens because PRDs and tech docs drift out of date. For a side panel with many states, she made a grid of every permutation, from most collapsed to most expanded. She keeps the designs pixel perfect with system variables, so Dev Mode gives correct specs. Her notes cover only what Dev Mode can't, like 'not needed for milestone one'.",
     "t": 1108
    },
    {
     "title": "Storyboard motion with keyframes and exact numbers",
     "detail": "A startup designer drew a transition storyboard for an assistant that grows from a button to a pill to a chat. It shows made-up in-between frames, the math ('expands to 343 pixels', the pill moves down by half the padding), and which elements fade in when. He also shared Figma and Principle prototypes. OnCourse could spec the answer reveal or streak animation this way.",
     "t": 824
    },
    {
     "title": "Explain the why, every state and odd spacing",
     "detail": "A Figma designer's spec starts with an overview and why it is being built. It covers states, hover and click, long and short text, localization, and empty and error states. She calls out spacing that breaks the grid, like a 2px gap, because Dev Mode won't flag it. After staging feedback she adds a dated page with what changed and keeps the old one for context.",
     "t": 297
    },
    {
     "title": "Split the intended design from release one",
     "detail": "Firefox's address bar spec covers secure and insecure sites, reader mode, long URLs, top and bottom bars, landscape, iPad, and screen-reader order. Pages hold the ideal 'intended design'. After talking with engineering, they make a separate first-release version without the hard parts, like translucency. This keeps the vision without blocking shipping.",
     "t": 589
    }
   ],
